---
/**
 * /about — WHO DIRECTHIRED IS. Not why to choose it.
 *
 * Core-pages design spec §3.2 gives it five parts and no others: company
 * story · philosophy · Singapore presence · credentials with the verified
 * MOM licence · CTA. Spec §4 gives it brand and trust intent, which it
 * shares with /why-directhired.
 *
 * THE DIVISION OF LABOUR WITH /why-directhired, WHICH IS THIS PAGE'S ONE
 * REAL PROBLEM. Both pages are trust pages, both draw on the same small
 * pool of supplied facts, and a visitor who reads both must not feel they
 * read one page twice. The split adopted, and it is a hard line:
 *
 *   /why-directhired ARGUES. It is the case for choosing DirectHired —
 *   the three pillars at length, the consequence that there is no gallery
 *   to browse, the two-sided match, and a credentials block whose job is
 *   to close that argument ("Everything above is a description of how we
 *   work. These three are not.").
 *
 *   /about STATES. It is the company as an entity: what it is, what it
 *   holds to, where it operates from, what it is licensed to do and how
 *   long it has done it. Nothing on this page argues a case; the one
 *   sentence that would have is a link to the page that does.
 *
 * Concretely, that is what keeps the two credentials blocks from being the
 * same block twice. /why-directhired publishes three facts as EVIDENCE,
 * each with the scope it was measured over spelled out, under a heading
 * that tells the reader to check them. This page publishes the company's
 * PARTICULARS, as a register entry — including the registered entity, which
 * that page does not carry and which is the fact an About page exists to
 * give. Same values, single-sourced from src/data/company.ts so they cannot
 * disagree; different jobs.
 *
 * THE FOUNDING LINE IS SOURCE BRIEF §35, AND ONLY ITS ORIGIN SENTENCE.
 * Spec §3.4 records that sentence, by name, as the About founding story —
 * a decision taken BY DEFAULT because DirectHired has not supplied their
 * own words, and §9 still lists "founding story in DirectHired's words" as
 * an open input. So it ships as written: no founder, no year of insight,
 * no anecdote. /why-directhired carries all three of §35's lines because
 * §35 is that page's whole origin block; this page takes the one sentence
 * §3.4 assigns to it, which is also why the overlap is a sentence rather
 * than a section. When DirectHired supplies their own account, BOTH change.
 *
 * THE COMPANY, NOT ITS FOUNDERS — master brief §45, and it is a hard rule
 * rather than a preference. There is no named founder here, no photograph,
 * no personal history and no "our team of experts", because every one of
 * those needs a fact nobody has supplied. The one thing the brief itself
 * says about people — that the company was established because "the
 * founders saw families struggling" — is deliberately NOT written as a
 * founder's story: §45 asks for a trusted company rather than a
 * personal-founder brand, and §35's sentence is the same event told the
 * way the client approved it.
 *
 * NO IMAGERY, and that is on the record before anyone reaches for some.
 * src/data/images.json registers a `team-and-office` slot with no consumer
 * and aiPermitted: false, whose stated reason is exactly this page: "a
 * generated office or generated staff photograph is a claim about a real,
 * licensed business (MOM 23C1443) that is simply false… the prohibition is
 * on record before anyone adds an About block and reaches for a plausible-
 * looking interior." Real photography is Priority 2 of the shot brief and
 * sub-project 4's work.
 *
 * NO MOM CREST, SEAL OR MARK — open decision D-7, unchanged. Whether
 * DirectHired may display one is a permission nobody in this repository
 * holds. A licence number set in type is safe; an official-looking mark on
 * the page whose whole subject is the licence is exactly the wrong place to
 * find out.
 *
 * THE SINGAPORE BLOCK IS NOT /contact. Task 10 owns the contact treatment
 * and this page must not pre-empt it: no phone, no email, no hours, no
 * socials, no map, and no link to /contact (which does not exist yet and is
 * not in tests/links.test.ts's DEFERRED_ROUTES, so a link to it would fail
 * the suite as a dead link, correctly). What ships is the one fact that
 * belongs to the company's identity rather than to getting in touch: it is
 * a Singapore business, and the office is in Singapore. The address string
 * is assembled from src/data/company.ts exactly as Footer.astro assembles
 * it — never retyped.
 *
 * NO SECOND DARK BAND. Spec §6 keeps block 07's --color-deep as the site's
 * only palette register shift, and §3.2 does not list TwoSidedMatch in this
 * page's composition — so it is not rendered here, for the same reason
 * /find-your-helper declined it. /why-directhired renders it because §3.2
 * names it there; that reasoning does not reach this page.
 *
 * Section grounds run cream / white / teal / white / cream across the five
 * blocks (.about-company, .about-philosophy, .about-singapore, .about-record,
 * FinalCta), registered and asserted in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts. The
 * brand wash lands on the Singapore block: --color-surface-teal exists to
 * give #00a4a6 real graphic area, and the block about where the company IS
 * is the one place on this page a brand ground carries meaning rather than
 * decoration. The record block stays white, because a record reads as a
 * record on paper.
 *
 * NO INLINE CTA PAIR, unlike /why-directhired and /find-your-helper. Those
 * two run long enough that a reader meets an argument's conclusion far
 * above the foot of the page. This page is four short blocks and FinalCta
 * sits directly under the record; a second pair of buttons a few hundred
 * pixels above it would put two conversion surfaces in one screen. The
 * mobile CTA bar is fixed on every page regardless.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import FinalCta from '../sections/FinalCta.astro'
import { company } from '../data/company'

/*
 * Assembled from company.ts the same way Footer.astro assembles it. The
 * address is not retyped here — it is one of the "consistent company
 * details" master brief §51 asks for, and two copies of an address is how
 * a move half-lands.
 */
const addressLine = `${company.address.street}, ${company.address.locality} ${company.address.postalCode}`

/*
 * Both the title and the description resolve the licence from company.ts
 * rather than typing it. That is not fussiness: the literal in
 * /why-directhired's description was a second copy of a regulator-issued
 * identifier sitting where nobody sees it rendered, and it is now guarded
 * site-wide in tests/company.test.ts. The title carries the brand name and
 * the licence because this page owns spec §4's brand-and-trust intent, and
 * "directhired mom licence" is the shape those searches take.
 */
const title = `About DirectHired — Singapore Maid Agency, MOM Licence ${company.momLicence}`
const description =
  `${company.registeredName} is a licensed Singapore maid agency placing domestic helpers with ` +
  `households here since ${company.foundedYear}. MOM Employment Agency licence ${company.momLicence}.`
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} breadcrumb="About">
  <section class="about-company">
    <Container>
      {/*
        The <h1> states the identity rather than making a claim, because
        that is the question this page answers. /why-directhired opens on
        an argument ("We don't just fill vacancies."); this opens on a
        fact, and the difference between the two openings is the whole
        division of labour in one line.
      */}
      <h1>DirectHired is a Singapore employment agency.</h1>

      {/*
        SOURCE BRIEF §35's ORIGIN SENTENCE, AS WRITTEN. Spec §3.4 assigns
        exactly this sentence to the About page. Do not embroider it, do
        not name a founder and do not add a year of insight: it is a
        default decision, taken because DirectHired has not given us their
        own account, and an invented detail here would read as history and
        be the hardest kind to remove later.
      */}
      {/*
        SOURCED, NOT RETYPED (2026-08-17). This was a hardcoded literal, as
        was the identical sentence on /why-directhired, while
        src/sections/Difference.astro published a paraphrase of it. Spec §3.4
        requires the brief's wording verbatim and spec §9 has DirectHired's
        own account replacing it, so it is now single-sourced from
        src/data/company.ts and all three surfaces read the one string.
      */}
      <p class="about-lede">{company.foundingStory}</p>

      <p class="about-body">
        It has been placing domestic helpers with households in Singapore since {company.foundedYear}.
      </p>

      {/*
        The division of labour, said to the visitor rather than only to the
        next author. A family that arrived here looking for the case gets
        sent to the page that makes it, on the first screen, instead of
        reading four blocks that decline to make it.
      */}
      <p class="about-body">
        This page is about the company itself — what it holds to, where it works from, and what is on
        record. The case for choosing it is made on the
        <a href="/why-directhired">why DirectHired</a> page.
      </p>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section class="about-philosophy">
    <Container>
      {/*
        Structural as well as editorial: the two statements below are
        <h3>s, so without this <h2> the document would run <h1> straight to
        <h3> and break its own outline. tests/pages.test.ts asserts it —
        heading-order is an axe BEST-PRACTICE rule and sits outside the
        wcag2a/wcag2aa tags scripts/run-axe.mjs filters to, so nothing else
        would catch it.
      */}
      <h2 class="section-title">What the company holds to</h2>

      <div class="tenets">
        {/*
          "Happy Employer. Happy Helper." IS DirectHired's philosophy, in
          the brief's own words: master brief §1 introduces it as "DirectHired's
          core philosophy is:". Spec §3.2 gives this page a philosophy
          section, and a philosophy section that declines to state the
          philosophy would be a strange thing to ship.

          IT APPEARS ONCE, HERE, WHICH IS THE RULE. Brief §3 warns against
          turning the line into a slogan repeated on every block, and the
          brand assessment states it as "appears once on the whole page".
          This page renders no TwoSidedMatch (spec §3.2 does not list it),
          so this is the page's single appearance — and it is a different
          use from block 07's, which is a composition about the outcome of
          a match. Here it is quoted as what the company stands for. If a
          future change ever puts TwoSidedMatch on this page, this heading
          comes out in the same commit.

          Both sides mattering is supplied, not inferred: brief §44's core
          themes include "We care about both employer and helper", and
          block 07 already resolves on "Better matching happens when both
          sides are understood."
        */}
        <div class="tenet">
          <h3>Happy Employer. Happy Helper.</h3>
          <p>
            DirectHired’s own statement of what a placement is for. There are two people in a
            placement, and it has to work for the household and for the helper — not for one of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        {/*
          The second half of brief §1: "DirectHired takes the opposite
          approach: Understand first. Recommend second."

          ONE SENTENCE, 2026-08-16, DOWN FROM TWO. It used to add "The
          household is understood before any helper is put forward", which
          did two things and neither of them well: it paraphrased the
          heading directly above it, and it restated /why-directhired's "We
          go through the household before we suggest anyone" in different
          words. Now that both pages exist, a reader meets the same claim
          twice — and this page's whole discipline is that it STATES while
          that page ARGUES. So the argument's sentence stays there and this
          keeps only what this page can say about it: that the order is
          DirectHired's own.

          It is shorter than the tenet beside it and that is not an
          oversight. What this order looks like in practice — the list of
          what is understood before a recommendation, and the consequence
          that there is no gallery of helpers to browse — is
          /why-directhired's Better Matching pillar, argued at length, and
          the link on the first screen already points at it.
        */}
        <div class="tenet">
          <h3>Understand first. Recommend second.</h3>
          <p>The order the work runs in, in DirectHired’s own words.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section class="about-singapore">
    <Container>
      <h2 class="section-title">Based in Singapore</h2>

      {/*
        Master brief §51 asks the site to communicate Singapore presence
        with consistent company details. This is that, and no more than
        that: the address comes from company.ts, and the phone, email,
        hours and socials are deliberately absent because /contact (Task
        10) owns them. Nothing here characterises the district, describes
        the office or counts the staff — none of which anybody has
        supplied, and all of which are the natural next sentence.
      */}
      {/*
        Two columns from --bp-tablet up, prose left and the address right.
        Single-column, the wash ran 546px tall at 1280 with its content in
        the left 60% and nothing beside it — measured in Chrome — which
        makes the one section on this page that spends the brand ground
        look like the one section that had nothing to put in it. The
        address is a fact rather than a sentence, so it earns the second
        column and gets the site's eyebrow treatment above it.
      */}
      <div class="presence">
        <div class="presence-copy">
          {/*
            "…and so is the office" — full stop, 2026-08-16.

            It read "and so is the office those placements are handled from",
            which inferred two things from an address string: that there is
            ONE office, and that placements are handled there. Nobody has
            supplied either. The clause is the natural next sentence and
            that is exactly why it had to go — an office an About page
            describes is an office somebody will later be asked about.
          */}
          <p class="about-body">
            DirectHired is a Singapore business. The families it works with are here, the helpers it
            places work in households here, and so is the office.
          </p>

          <p class="about-body">
            What the company is licensed to do here is a matter of public record rather than of
            description. That record is below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="presence-office">
          <p class="presence-label">Office</p>
          <p class="about-address">{addressLine}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section class="about-record">
    <Container>
      <h2 class="section-title">The company on record</h2>
      {/*
        "as the company gives them", not "as registered and published",
        2026-08-16.

        "Registered" was untrue of the track-record row — a count of
        placements is registered nowhere — and it presumed a registration
        nobody in this repository has read. Every value in this block comes
        from DirectHired: the entity name and the UEN were confirmed by them
        on 2026-08-16, the licence number was supplied with the brief, and
        the count is their own basis sentence. src/data/company.ts records
        that verification was attempted for the first two and failed.

        Saying so is not a hedge on a trust page. The one value a family can
        independently check is the licence, and the note beside it already
        says where: MOM's public register.
      */}
      <p class="record-lede">DirectHired’s particulars, as the company gives them.</p>

      {/*
        EVERY VALUE COMES FROM src/data/company.ts. Not one is retyped:
        master brief §70 for the licence and the entity it is held in
        (never invent, modify, guess or substitute; verify against the
        official MOM source before publication), and §68 for the count,
        which was revised down from "1,000+" once already. Both rules are
        enforced across every surface by tests/company.test.ts.

        A ROW EACH, not the three-abreast grid /why-directhired uses. That
        page's block is an argument's evidence and gives each fact the
        scope it was measured over in a paragraph; this is a register
        entry, so it takes the site's established fact-table shape — term
        in a fixed column, value beside it, hairline between rows, the
        same treatment /find-your-helper gives its timings.
      */}
      <dl class="record">
        <div class="record-row">
          <dt>Registered entity</dt>
          <dd class="record-value">{company.registeredName}</dd>
          {/*
            The fact an About page exists to give and the one credential
            /why-directhired does not carry: the legal person behind the
            brand name. Master brief §45 wants a trusted COMPANY rather
            than a personal-founder brand, and a registered entity is the
            most literal form that takes.
          */}
          <dd class="record-note">The company the DirectHired name belongs to.</dd>
        </div>

        {/*
          THE UEN, supplied by DirectHired on 2026-08-16 (spec §2.6.9) and
          published because a UEN is the usual companion to a registered
          name on a Singapore About page: it is the handle a family or a
          regulator looks the company up BY, where the name is only what
          they read back. Read from company.ts like every other value here —
          see that file's docblock for what is and is not verified about it.
        */}
        <div class="record-row">
          <dt>UEN</dt>
          <dd class="record-value">{company.uen}</dd>
          <dd class="record-note">
            The company’s Unique Entity Number, as issued on incorporation in Singapore.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="record-row">
          <dt>MOM licence</dt>
          <dd class="record-value">{company.momLicence}</dd>
          <dd class="record-note">
            Employment Agency licence, issued by the Ministry of Manpower and listed in its public
            register.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="record-row">
          <dt>Placing helpers since</dt>
          <dd class="record-value">{company.foundedYear}</dd>
        </div>

        <div class="record-row">
          <dt>Track record</dt>
          {/*
            DirectHired's own basis sentence, whole: "500+ placements
            across all services since 2022". "Placements", never "helpers
            placed" — that reads as new placements only, which is a
            smaller and stronger claim than this number supports, and a
            guard in tests/company.test.ts refuses it on every surface a
            visitor reads. A second guard asserts that every printing of
            the figure names what it counts, which is why the scope is in
            the value line rather than left to the note.
          */}
          <dd class="record-value">
            {company.placementCount} placements across all services since {company.foundedYear}
          </dd>
          <dd class="record-note">
            “All services” is DirectHired’s own scope for the figure: transfers, replacements and
            direct-hire processing are counted in it as well as new placements.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </Container>
  </section>

  {/*
    The page's one conversion surface, framed for this page: a record is
    the part of a company that can be published, and the part that cannot
    is what a first submission actually starts. "A consultant reads every
    submission" is already published site copy — master brief line 353's
    intake role, which the FAQ states — and it deliberately stops short of
    a response time, which the FAQ owns and states as within 1 business day.
  */}
  <FinalCta
    heading="Start with your requirements."
    lede="Send us what your household needs. A consultant reads every submission, and that is where working with DirectHired begins."
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-company {
    /*
     * --space-12 top, --space-section bottom — Hero's treatment, and the
     * treatment /pricing, /find-your-helper and /why-directhired all give
     * their opening block, for the same reason: this block carries the
     * page's hero, and a full --space-section (128px at ≥1280) above an
     * image-less single-column headline spends the first screen on cream.
     */
    padding-block: var(--space-12) var(--space-section);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .about-company h1 {
    margin: 0;
    /*
     * 20ch, not the 16–18ch the other page heroes use, and it was measured
     * rather than guessed. This headline is one 45-character sentence; at
     * 18ch it set as three lines in real Chrome at 1280 with "DirectHired"
     * alone on the first, which reads as a label above a heading rather
     * than as the sentence it is. 20ch (819px at --size-h1's 64px) breaks
     * it once, after "Singapore", and holds at 1920 because the display
     * size does not grow past 1280. Re-measure if --size-h1 changes.
     *
     * ASSERTED in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts. Reverting this line to 16ch
     * used to score 455/455 green — nothing in the suite read `20ch` at all —
     * which is how a number that cost a browser session comes back. Change
     * the test in the same commit as the value, or not at all.
     */
    max-width: 20ch;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h1);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Brief §35's sentence, in full ink at lede size: it is the reason the
     company exists and the only line on this page a visitor is likely to
     read word for word rather than scan. */
  .about-lede {
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .about-body {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* --color-accent (6.03:1 on cream, 5.83:1 on white, 5.54:1 on the teal
     wash), never --color-brand-teal, which measures 2.89:1 and carries no
     text anywhere on this site. */
  .about-body a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  .about-body a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    border-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .about-philosophy {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* White, between the cream hero above and the teal wash below.
       Registered in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts; the grounds have to be
       judged as a sequence, so do not flip this one alone. */
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  /* Shared by all three page-local <h2>s, at --size-h3 rather than
     --size-h2: these head a sub-part of a page the <h1> has already
     opened, and setting them at section-header scale would put three
     competing display sizes on one screen. Same size and role as
     /pricing's `.grid-heading` and /why-directhired's `.section-title`. */
  .section-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .tenets {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  /*
   * Opened by a hairline in --color-border (decorative, ~1.3:1 — see
   * tokens.css; it carries no text and bounds no control), never
   * --color-brand-teal. Teal as a drawn line is reserved for exactly two
   * places on this site, block 07's crossbar and FinalCta's mark, and
   * spending it on routine section furniture is what makes those two stop
   * meaning anything.
   */
  .tenet {
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* Fraunces at 1.25rem — the same treatment /why-directhired gives a
     pillar title. These two headings are quoted lines rather than labels,
     so they are set as display type and not as tracked eyebrows. */
  .tenet h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .tenet p {
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .about-singapore {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /*
     * The page's one section in the brand wash, and the only ground here
     * that is a choice rather than a consequence. --color-surface-teal is
     * 8% --color-brand-teal over cream (tokens.css) and exists to give the
     * logo colour real graphic AREA without ever setting text in it;
     * measured, --color-ink reads 12.87:1 on it and --color-ink-muted
     * 7.32:1. The block about where the company physically is is the one
     * place on this page where a brand ground means something. One per
     * page is the rule — tests/section-rhythm.test.ts asserts it.
     */
    background: var(--color-surface-teal);
  }

  .about-singapore .about-body {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    /* Full ink rather than --color-ink-muted on this ground: the wash
       lifts the page's lightness, and the muted step that reads as
       secondary on white starts to read as faded here. */
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .presence {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .presence-copy {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* The copy paragraphs are inside a grid that spaces them, so the
     stacking margin they carry elsewhere on this page would double it. */
  .presence-copy .about-body {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* The site's established eyebrow treatment, same as .record dt and
     TrustBadge's .trust-label. --tracking-wide's contract requires
     text-transform: uppercase in the same rule (tests/tokens.test.ts). */
  .presence-label {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /*
   * The address at display size — it is a fact rather than prose, and it
   * is the one thing in this block a visitor might copy. Tabular figures
   * for the same reason TrustBadge uses them: a postal code should read
   * as a number.
   */
  .about-address {
    max-width: 30ch;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .about-record {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* White, between the teal wash above and FinalCta's cream below.
       FinalCta is fixed at --color-surface because the homepage renders it
       too, which is what fixes this one. */
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  .record-lede {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .record {
    max-width: 60rem;
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 0;
  }

  /*
   * A term/value pair per row with the rule between rows rather than
   * around them — the shape /find-your-helper gives its timings, and for
   * the same reason: a card each would box four short facts into four
   * surfaces on a white ground (the generic-UI-kit reading Card.astro's
   * own header warns about), and a paragraph each would bury the values,
   * which are what a visitor is in this block for.
   */
  .record-row {
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .record-row:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* The site's established eyebrow treatment (TrustBadge's .trust-label):
     small, semibold, uppercase, tracked. --tracking-wide's contract
     requires text-transform: uppercase in the same rule — asserted in
     tests/tokens.test.ts, which scans src/pages too. */
  .record dt {
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* Figtree, not Fraunces, and tabular figures — the same treatment
     TrustBadge and /why-directhired's credentials give these values, so a
     licence number reads as a number rather than as display copy. */
  .record-value {
    max-width: 40ch;
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .record-note {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* 48em = --bp-tablet (tokens.css); the literal is repeated by hand
     because custom properties do not resolve inside a media condition. */
  @media (min-width: 48em) {
    .tenets {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }

    /* Prose left, the address right. `minmax(0, 1fr)` on the first track
       lets it shrink below its content's min-content width instead of
       pushing the row wider than the container.

       ASSERTED in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts. Deleting this rule returned
       the block to the measured 546px with its content in the left 60% and
       scored 455/455 green — nothing in the suite read `presence` at all. */
    .presence {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 20rem;
      column-gap: var(--space-12);
      align-items: start;
    }

    /*
     * The term column is fixed rather than fluid so all four values start
     * on one axis; `minmax(0, 1fr)` on the second track lets it shrink
     * below its content's min-content width instead of pushing the row
     * wider than the container.
     *
     * The dt is pinned to the first cell and every dd to the second, in
     * explicit placement rather than auto-flow: a row with a value AND a
     * note auto-places the note back under the term in column 1, which
     * fills the term column with body copy — the layout this row shape
     * exists to avoid.
     */
    .record-row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 16rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: var(--space-8);
      align-items: baseline;
    }

    .record dt {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    .record dd {
      grid-column: 2;
    }

    .record-value {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
